<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Local DEVELOPMENT entitlements for the native Mac target.

  RULE APPLIED HERE: a permission and an entitlement are different things, and a
  privacy usage description is not an entitlement. Only capabilities the code
  actually exercises are requested. Each omission below is deliberate.

  Companion file: macos/Resources/Info.plist
  Built by:       macos/Scripts/package-mac-app.sh (ad-hoc identity, `codesign -s -`)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The ONE entitlement requested, and the only one Xcode itself attaches to a
	  locally built bundle: it lets a debugger attach to the process. This is a
	  development convenience for a bundle that exists to be run locally and
	  debugged - it is not a TCC permission, and it grants the user no access to
	  anything. It is signed with an ad-hoc identity, so it confers no trust.

	  DELIBERATELY ABSENT, each justified:

	  com.apple.security.app-sandbox
	    Not sandboxed. The target writes under the user's home, reaches a
	    caller-supplied base URL over HTTP, and uses the login Keychain via
	    SessionKeychain.swift. Sandboxing would require further entitlements for
	    all three, and would change the security posture of the native app for no
	    development benefit. A local development bundle is the one case where
	    running unsandboxed is appropriate.

	  com.apple.security.network.client / .network.server
	    Entitlement-only when sandboxed. Unsandboxed processes need no network
	    entitlement; MusterTransport's GET-only probe works without one.

	  keychain-access-groups
	    SessionKeychain uses the default access group of an unsandboxed app, which
	    needs no declared group. Declaring one would place dev credentials in a
	    shared group - the opposite of what a development bundle should do.

	  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit, .allow-unsigned-executable-memory
	    Nothing in this Swift target generates or executes code at runtime, so
	    there is no JIT and no unsigned executable memory to permit. The shipped
	    Electron app needs both because Chromium's V8 requires them; copying that
	    pair here would grant an exception this binary never uses.

	  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
	    The Electron app needs this to load its own unsigned helper bundles. This
	    binary loads no unsigned plugin or bundle - only system frameworks and a
	    statically linked CompanionCore - so the exception is unnecessary and
	    would weaken library validation for no reason.

	  com.apple.security.device.audio-input,
	  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events, and any TCC capability
	    Entitlement-only under the sandbox. The code requests no microphone,
	    screen capture, accessibility or Apple Events access at all, so there is
	    no capability to declare. The matching NS*UsageDescription strings are
	    absent from Info.plist for the same reason.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
